Z||000004||JohnnyK||09-19-2000||03:57 PM||||I always wanted to put a turbo on my 2.5, but everyone said it would be way too much work to beef up the duck.... But anyone know how those turbos would fit on a 3.4 (what mods it would need). Just curious||||reg||1
Z||000005||RedOktober||09-20-2000||02:15 AM||||I've considered strapping the 2.2 turbo onto the duck but decided against it for the time being for the simple reason (Huge requirement of $$$$$$) <BR>but if you are interested I have almost all the info requred to do this. <BR>1)exhaust manifold must be modified. The stock manifod could work with a bit of modification to the gasket area you need to make a flange to mount the turbo. This also alows the placement of the O2 sensor. Also, if the turbo is from an 87 car, you will need to get it rebuilt ( total Aprox $500- 800)<BR>2) the Garrett t03 turbo works great if you just rotate the downpipe and re weld it back into the factor down pipe.<BR>3) you need to order the Holley TB, with the Jeep injector(better flow rate) An after market Fuel Pressure Regulator that is boost dependent.( $750-1000 depending on the system)<BR>4) you need to fab a Pressure hat that will seal with the TB. This is the fun part. It cannot be too small yet it cannot be too big(loss of boost).( $200-500 if you can find someone who will try )<BR>5) you will need a stand alone EFI system that will piggy back onto the factory EFI system and replace the distributor for your ignition system. This is the most fun part because there are no aftermarket chips available for the 2.5, so you have to compensate.( $650-1200) try <A HREF="http://www.electromotive-inc.com" TARGET=_blank>www.electromotive-inc.com</A> <BR>6) you have to rebuild your engine and pray that your crank will hold up toi the extra stresses that the turbo puts on it. New rings and all that fun stuff. It is possible to us Chev 305 pistons(deep dished ) for extra boost and a lower compression ratio. ($1000-1800 Depends on you and your core and how high tech you want to go ) <BR>7) a good buddy with a dyno that will let you remap you EFI while you are making multiple runs trying to figure out all the requirements needed for daily driving. (not needed for race only applications, or needed more for race only, you be the judge!)( $500Plus) <BR>8) if you get all this done, you are almost better off going out and buying a fully loaded V6 GT or SE and putting a turbo onto that. ( which is what I did) Figured I saved $3000.<P>Sorry this is so long, but Ive found very few peeps interested in this and I wanted to share what Ive learned.<P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater)<p>[This message has been edited by RedOktober (edited 09-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Xtreme Fiero's||09-20-2000||08:49 AM||||you must cut off the end of your exhaust manifold and weld on a flange, also drill for a O2 sensor. my car came with a special sleeve it mount the turbo to the manilold (lots of leaks) It also came with a G20 turbo Thies are WAY to big for a duke thier recomended for 15-20 PSI (street) 25 PSI (race). i would use a g16 eclipse/galant VR-4 turbo (15PSI max) and use a HKS opo off valve. the G16 also has a preset wastegate (10-15 PSI) If you have any more questions please just ask. I will soon me selling 2.5 exhaust manifolds w/ turbo flanges.||||reg||1
